Z||000000||Formula||09-27-2000||10:16 PM||||a 225/60-15 is 225 mm from bead to bead with 60% of the 225 being sidewall, and the 15 is for a 15" rim.....<P>storytime, in electronics engineering 101 today at penn state almost the whole class had a discussion before the class about what the tire size numbers mean.  I said that it is 225 mm for the tread width (which is wrong) and 60% of that size is sidewall... all of the older people in the class said that 225 is the height of the tire and 60 is the width. which is wrong too.... so my friend and I went to montgomery ward and went to the tire department to get the facts.... well, the 35 year veteran of tires said that 225 is tire height and 60 is the series which is width.....we both knew that was clearly wrong and humored him. (he also said that my fiero was engineered wrong because the car should sit level and if it has 205s on the front and 215s on the back then its not correct) so....... in the search for the correct calculations, we went to a local performance tire store and they explained that it is 225 mm from bead to bead (he also pointed out the beads) and that the side wall is 60% of the 225.  he also copied a paper that explained it for us to take back to school......we're taking the paper to mont. wards on friday.... my question is.... how could a person who has sold tires for 30+ years not know what the numbers on the sidewall mean? I knew what he said was wrong because vipers come with like 330/30-18s or something big like that and that would be taller than most truck tires according to what he said.||||reg||1
