Z||000004||HugGRRRFiero||09-27-2000||11:00 PM||||Yea, you'd think he'd know after being in a tire shop for 35 years.  To be honest I thought the first number was the width like you did until someone told me it's from bead to bead not too long ago.<P>That's hard to believe the guy didn't know...<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||gixxer||09-27-2000||11:02 PM||||The smart guy at the performance store is wrong...Let's split hairs.....<BR>If a tire is, say, a 215/60R-15, the 215 is the absolute total section width of the tire as measured on a given rim width(the design rim width). Typically the tire sidewall will overhang the rim slightly. This enhances the use of the tire as a functional part of the cars' suspension system. If the rim width is wider than the design rim width, the tire section width will be slightly wider also. If the rim is very wide, then it will be the tires section width, and the tire probably won't ride as well as the skinny rim setup.  The "60" is the aspect ratio. The ratio of the section width multiplied, in this case, by .6, giving you the the sidewall height for 1/2 the tire. <BR>A tire this size MUST meet federal load carrying standards for its size, so that another brand of tire with the same size will more or less match up. However, I believe there is an allowed 5% tolerance in the tires' physical dimensions. The tread width can very considerably from brand to brand, so can internal construction, leading to tires of the same size, from different manufacturers, handling differently.<BR>Gain bonus points in your class for pointing out that tires serve as static electricity dissipaters(the carbon black helps here). Automobiles in motion (airplanes, helicopters too) tend to remove electrons off air molecules, thus building up a static charge. <BR>But this is why you were on the tire subject in the first place, right?      ||||reg||1
Z||000006||HugGRRRFiero||09-27-2000||11:31 PM||||gixxer is right, it's the total section width.  I was thinking bead as the radius that "pooches" the sidwall giving the overall section width and not the width of the bead at the rim.  That's right, the guy at the tire store is wrong, and I need to think about what I'm typing before I type.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/redface.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Big O||09-28-2000||12:18 AM||||According to the professors at Big O University, the 225 is the width of the tire from sidewall to sidewall (at the widest point) with the tire fully inflated to the specified pressure, but with no load on it.  The 60 is the aspect ratio which is the percentage of sidewall to tread width.  Thus, when you increase the tread width and maintain the same aspect ratio, the overall diameter of the tire will increase.  This is how you get "zero" sizing which can give you two tires of the same overall diameter, but with different widths.  So... 225 is the tire width, 60 is the percentage of sidewall to tread, and 15 is the wheel diameter. A tire that is 245 60 15 will be both wider and taller than a 225 60 15.  This is why you decrease the aspect ratio to maintain the overall tire diameter when you want to go to a larger size wheel.||||reg||1
Z||000008||theogre||09-28-2000||07:51 PM||||What O said about keeping rolling diameter is right.<P>most of the tire maker web sites explain the size numbers in detail.... The tire spec's available on those sites usually include the rim width the tire numbers were calculated on and what range of rims the tire can be mounted.<P>To pull it all together in short.<P>195/70R14 90T<P>195 Tire section width at widest point of sidewalls (usually calculated on 6" wide rim for this size.)<BR>70 aspect ratio. wall is this percent of section width.<BR>R radial<BR>14 inside diameter of bead<BR>90 load rating<BR>T speed rating<P>Tread width is not printed on a tire. tread width for a given size can varry an inch or more from one type/brand to another.<P>The letter ratings for the tread life, traction and temperature are based on DOT guidlines. NHTSA is considering revising these numbers in light of the ford/firestone tire recall. If that is done the new guidelines should show up in a couple years. maybe.<P>Rim width is the width between the bead flanges. not total width. test/design width is pretty standard for each size. The examle above is mounted on a 6" rim in every set of spec's I've seen.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 09-28-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||CW85T-Type||09-28-2000||08:09 PM||||FWIW<P>To calculate tire height<BR>W = Width (205)<BR>A = Aspect (60)<BR>R = Rim Height (15)<BR>((((W/25.4)*A)*2)+R)<P>So that would be<P>205 / 25.4 = 8.07 (convert MM to inches)<BR>8.07 * 60% = 4.842 (height of one sidewall)<BR>4.842 * 2 = 9.684 (tires have 2 sidewalls  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">)<BR>9.685 + 15 = 24.684 (the tire height)<P>Chris Wilson||||reg||10
